The proposed answer is incorrect. While it gets the date (October 20), location, commanders, and general significance right, the Battle of Navarino took place in **1827**, not 1828. 

The Treaty of London was signed on July 6, 1827, and the Battle of Navarino occurred just a few months later on October 20, 1827—not over a year later as the proposed answer claims.

{"verdict": "incorrect", "confidence": 100, "correct_answer": "1827"}

The proposed answer is incorrect. While it gets many of the technical and historical details right (such as the probe's design, landing location, weight, and the interception of signals by Jodrell Bank), it gets the year wrong and fabricates a timeline around Sergei Korolev's death to justify the error. 

Luna 9 was launched on January 31, 1966, and achieved the first successful soft landing on the Moon on **February 3, 1966** (shortly *after* Sergei Korolev's death on January 14, 1966, meaning he did not live to see the success). The failed probes launched late in 1965 were Luna 7 and Luna 8.

{"verdict": "incorrect", "confidence": 100, "correct_answer": "1966"}

The proposed answer is incorrect. While all of the supporting historical details in the answer are accurate—including the designer (Isambard Kingdom Brunel), the captain (James Anderson), the modifications made to the ship, and the dates and locations of the voyage—they describe the **SS *Great Eastern***, not the SS *Great Western*. 

The SS *Great Western* was an earlier wooden-hulled paddle steamer also designed by Brunel (launched in 1837 and scrapped in 1856), whereas the SS *Great Eastern* (launched in 1858) was the massive iron-hulled steamship that laid the 1866 transatlantic telegraph cable.

{"verdict": "incorrect", "confidence": 100, "correct_answer": "SS Great Eastern"}

The proposed answer is incorrect. While Bell Labs did have its main research headquarters in Murray Hill (which is an unincorporated community within Berkeley Heights and New Providence, not a township itself), the horn antenna used by Arno Penzias and Robert Wilson was not located there. 

Known as the **Holmdel Horn Antenna**, the instrument was actually located at the Bell Labs Crawford Hill facility in **Holmdel Township**, Monmouth County, New Jersey. 

{"verdict": "incorrect", "confidence": 100, "correct_answer": "Holmdel Township"}

