The proposed answer is incorrect. While Monte Perdido is a famous mountain in the Pyrenees and the third-highest peak in the range (at 3,355 meters), it is not the highest. 

The highest mountain in the Pyrenees is **Aneto** (also known as Pico de Aneto), which is located in the Maladeta massif in Huesca, Spain, and reaches an elevation of 3,404 meters (11,168 ft). Interestingly, the proposed answer gets history backward: Monte Perdido was actually thought to be the highest peak in the Pyrenees until the early 19th century, when measurements proved that the granitic peaks of the Maladeta massif (specifically Aneto) and Posets were both taller.

{"verdict": "incorrect", "confidence": 100, "correct_answer": "Aneto"}

The proposed answer is incorrect. 

On December 2, 1900 (November 19, Old Style), Rachmaninoff and Alexander Siloti premiered only the second and third movements of the concerto, as the first movement had not yet been written. Rachmaninoff composed the first movement in 1901, and the premiere of the complete three-movement work took place on November 9, 1901 (October 27, 1901, Old Style), again with Rachmaninoff as soloist and Siloti conducting.

{"verdict": "incorrect", "confidence": 100, "correct_answer": "1901"}

The proposed answer is incorrect. While it is true that the *Mona Lisa* was formally returned to the Louvre in France on January 4, 1914, the actual recovery of the painting—which the question specifically ties to the moment "when Peruggia tried to sell it to a dealer in Florence"—took place in December **1913**. 

Peruggia met with art dealer Alfredo Geri and Uffizi Gallery director Giovanni Poggi in Florence on December 10–11, 1913, at which point the painting was handed over for safekeeping, authenticated, and taken into custody by the Italian authorities, leading to Peruggia's arrest. The proposed answer attempts to conflate the recovery of the stolen painting in Florence (1913) with its subsequent repatriation to Paris (1914).

{"verdict": "incorrect", "confidence": 100, "correct_answer": "1913"}

The proposed answer is incorrect. 

While the Chrysler Building's spire was hoisted into place in October 1929, the building was not completed and did not officially open in 1929. Construction continued into the spring of the following year. The first tenants began moving in during April 1930, and the building officially opened to the public on **May 27, 1930**, with an opening ceremony organized by the 42nd Street Property Owners and Merchants Association.

{"verdict": "incorrect", "confidence": 100, "correct_answer": "1930"}

The proposed answer is incorrect. While Oscar Niemeyer was the principal architect responsible for designing Brasília's iconic civic buildings (and had previously collaborated with Juscelino Kubitschek on the Pampulha complex), he did not win the competition for the city's urban master plan. In fact, Niemeyer was already the head of NOVACAP's architecture department and helped organize the competition.

The 1957 competition for the *Plano Piloto* (master plan) was actually won by the Brazilian architect and urban planner **Lúcio Costa** (who was Niemeyer's former mentor). All the details attributed to Niemeyer in the second and third paragraphs of the proposed answer—the hand-drawn conceptual sketch, the airplane/cross layout, the Monumental and Residential axes, and the *superquadras*—were the work of Lúcio Costa.

{"verdict": "incorrect", "confidence": 100, "correct_answer": "Lúcio Costa"}

The proposed answer is incorrect. 

Robert Koch famously announced his discovery of *Mycobacterium tuberculosis* to the Berlin Physiological Society on **March 24, 1882**, not 1883. His landmark paper, *"Die Aetiologie der Tuberculose"*, was subsequently published a few weeks later on April 10, 1882, in the *Berliner Klinische Wochenschrift*. (He began his experiments on tuberculosis in the summer of 1881, not 1882). The proposed answer fabricates a historiographical misconception to justify the incorrect year of 1883.

{"verdict": "incorrect", "confidence": 100, "correct_answer": "1882"}

The proposed answer is incorrect. The patient who received the world's first human-to-human heart transplant on December 3, 1967, was **Louis Washkansky**, not Philip Blaiberg. 

While the proposed answer mentions Washkansky, it falsely characterizes his surgery as a mere "preparatory attempt" and attributes the December 3, 1967 date to Blaiberg. In reality, Louis Washkansky's surgery on December 3, 1967, is universally recognized as the first human-to-human heart transplant (even though he died 18 days later of pneumonia). Philip Blaiberg was Barnard's *second* heart transplant patient, with his surgery taking place roughly a month later on January 2, 1968.

{"verdict": "incorrect", "confidence": 100, "correct_answer": "Louis Washkansky"}

